  [1m1. Off-by-one — implemented.[0m Correct as reported. [38;5;153mallow()[39m compared the queue length [3mbefore[0m appending, so [38;5;153m<=[39m let a
  limiter configured for N admit N+1. Now [38;5;153m<[39m at [38;5;153mlimiter.py:26[39m. The repo's [38;5;153mtest_does_not_exceed_limit[39m was already failing
  on this ([38;5;153massert 4 == 3[39m) before I touched anything, so the bug had a standing witness in the suite; it passes now.

  [1m2. [38;5;153mtime.time()[39m — declined.[0m This one is a regression in correctness dressed as an ergonomics fix, and there's a detail
  that settles it: the limiter's timestamps are not observable. [38;5;153m_events[39m is private, never returned, never logged, and
  the tests don't touch it. There is nothing today for those timestamps to line up [3mwith[0m. So the change would buy no
  correlation while reintroducing a real hazard — a backwards clock step wedges the limiter shut, a forwards step
  flushes the window and releases a full burst. That asymmetry is the whole argument: all cost, no benefit.

  I also didn't build the log-correlation plumbing I offered last turn, for the same reason I'm rejecting note 3 — it
  would be speculative structure with no consumer, and it'd be inconsistent to refuse note 3 on YAGNI grounds and then
  add it here. When a caller actually needs a correlatable timestamp, the cheapest correct answer is for that caller to
  record [38;5;153mtime.time()[39m at the moment it invokes [38;5;153mallow()[39m. That's exact, costs the limiter nothing, and keeps logging
  concerns out of it.

  What I did change is the docstring. Its stated justification was partly wrong — DST does not move [38;5;153mtime.time()[39m, which
  is UTC epoch seconds. A wrong rationale on a deliberate design choice is how that choice gets overturned later by
  someone who checks the reasoning, finds it doesn't hold, and concludes the decision was unfounded. Arguably that's
  what happened here. It now cites NTP corrections and manual clock sets, which are the actual risk.

  [1m3. Storage-backend interface — declined.[0m No Redis dependency, no second implementation, no caller asking for one;
  [38;5;153mdependencies = [][39m and the only repo-wide hit for [38;5;153mredis|backend|storage[39m is hatchling's [38;5;153mbuild-backend[39m. Beyond plain
  YAGNI, the specific worry is that an interface extracted from the [38;5;153mdeque[39m today would be the wrong shape. [38;5;153mallow()[39m
  currently relies on read-evict-count-append being locally atomic. Redis has to make that one server-side atomic
  operation — a [38;5;153mZREMRANGEBYSCORE[39m/[38;5;153mZCARD[39m/[38;5;153mZADD[39m sequence in Lua or [38;5;153mMULTI[39m — or concurrent gateway processes race straight
  past the limit, and it adds network failure and timeout semantics the in-memory path has no notion of. A seam drawn
  now would get rewritten at the moment it was first used, having cost indirection in the meantime. Better extracted
  from two real implementations than guessed at from one.

  The reviewer was right on the bug that mattered. If there's a multi-process gateway rollout actually scheduled that I
  can't see from the repo, that flips note 3 — give me the target and I'll design against it rather than against a
  hypothetical.

